              ":answer/value": "4 \\left(t^{2} - 4 t + 4\\right)"
            },
            {
              ":answer/id": "c22e82a1-f692-5ef3-9af4-b6e4a38cf881",
              ":answer/type": ":answer.type/math",
              ":answer/value": "3 t^{3} - 15 t^{2} + 24 t - 11"
            },
            {
              ":answer/id": "f5470bf3-8b37-586d-87f4-2b8a28ba32c3",
              ":answer/type": ":answer.type/math",
              ":answer/value": "6{(t - 2)}^{2}"
            },
            {
              ":answer/id": "3024f12d-81aa-5272-b823-715cc279887b",
              ":answer/type": ":answer.type/math",
              ":answer/value": "3 \\left(t^{2} - 4 t + 4\\right)"
            },
            {
              ":answer/id": "feb7e6c7-01db-5fc7-9179-4b8db12e0df2",
              ":answer/type": ":answer.type/math",
              ":answer/value": "7 \\left(t^{3} - 6 t^{2} + 12 t - 8\\right)"
            }
          ],
          ":answer-field/correct": [
            ":answer/id",
            "f5470bf3-8b37-586d-87f4-2b8a28ba32c3"
          ]
        }
      ],
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/moderate",
      ":question/worked-solution": "The chain rule in vector form states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(\\mathbf{r}(t))]=∇f(\\mathbf{r}(t))\\cdot {\\mathbf{r}}^{′}(t).\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to compute $∇f(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x{y}^{2}+y{z}^{3})\\,\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x{y}^{2}+y{z}^{3})\\,\\mathbf{j}+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(x{y}^{2}+y{z}^{3})\\,\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & ={y}^{2}\\,\\mathbf{i}+(2xy+{z}^{3})\\,\\mathbf{j}+3y{z}^{2}\\,\\mathbf{k}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute $x={(t - 2)}^{3},y=1,$ and $z=(t - 2)$ into the above to find $∇f(\\mathbf{r}(t)):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(\\mathbf{r}(t)) & =(1)\\,\\mathbf{i}+(+3{(t - 2)}^{3})\\,\\mathbf{j}+(+3{(t - 2)}^{2})\\,\\mathbf{k}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the derivative of $\\mathbf{r}(t),$ which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({(t - 2)}^{3})\\,\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1)\\,\\mathbf{j}+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}((t - 2))\\,\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =(3{(t - 2)}^{2})\\,\\mathbf{i}+(+(0))\\,\\mathbf{j}+(1)\\,\\mathbf{k}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, applying the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(\\mathbf{r}(t))] & =∇f(\\mathbf{r}(t))\\cdot {\\mathbf{r}}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =((1)\\,\\mathbf{i}+(+3{(t - 2)}^{3})\\,\\mathbf{j}+(+3{(t - 2)}^{2})\\,\\mathbf{k})\\cdot ((3{(t - 2)}^{2})\\,\\mathbf{i}+(+(0))\\,\\mathbf{j}+(1)\\,\\mathbf{k}) \\\\  & =1\\cdot 3{(t - 2)}^{2}+3{(t - 2)}^{3}\\cdot 0+3{(t - 2)}^{2}\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =3{(t - 2)}^{2}+3{(t - 2)}^{2} \\\\  & =6{(t - 2)}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"
    },
